ecvrf — provenance note
========================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bip340`/
`xeddsa`/`adaptor`/`frost`/`oscore`/`spake2plus` modules — self-contained
crypto modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

ecvrf — clean-room implementation of ECVRF-EDWARDS25519-SHA512-TAI, one
of the ciphersuites RFC 9381 defines. RFC 9381 (S. Goldberg, L. Reyzin,
D. Papadopoulos, J. Včelák, "Verifiable Random Functions (VRFs)", IRTF
CFRG, Informational, August 2023, ISSN 2070-1721,
https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9381.txt) is a public IRTF/IETF
specification with official test vectors (Appendix B.3) — a public
spec/RFC is not a copyrightable work (merger doctrine,
CONVENTIONS.md §5's "pure clean-room from spec, no NOTICE entry strictly
required" case); this file is kept anyway per this repository's
established practice of a module-local NOTICE for self-contained
crypto modules (mirroring `bip340`/`signal`'s precedent).

`ecvrf.zig` was written directly from RFC 9381 §5 (`ECVRF_prove`,
`ECVRF_proof_to_hash`, `ECVRF_verify`, and their §5.4 auxiliary
functions — `ECVRF_encode_to_curve_try_and_increment`,
`ECVRF_nonce_generation_RFC8032`, `ECVRF_challenge_generation`,
`ECVRF_decode_proof`, `ECVRF_validate_key`) and §5.5's
ECVRF-EDWARDS25519-SHA512-TAI parameter fixing (`suite_string = 0x03`,
`cLen = 16`, cofactor 8, little-endian `int_to_string`/`string_to_int`
per RFC 8032 §5.1.2), which in turn references RFC 8032 (S. Josefsson,
I. Liusvaara, "Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA)", IETF,
January 2017, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8032.txt) §5.1.2 (point
encoding — identical to `std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.toBytes`/
`fromBytes`) and §5.1.5 (secret-key expansion/clamping and public-key
derivation) for those two referenced pieces. No third-party ECVRF
implementation (including the reference implementation RFC 9381
Appendix B's own preamble names, https://github.com/reyzin/ecvrf, which
GENERATED the published test vectors but was not read or consulted
here) was read, ported, or consulted as a design reference for this
module's Zig code, types, or API shape — every construction detail in
`ecvrf.zig`'s doc comments was drawn from the two RFC texts themselves,
fetched directly from `rfc-editor.org` (the IETF's own canonical RFC
repository) for this pass.

`std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519` (+ its `scalar` submodule) and
`std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha512` (both MIT, part of Zig itself) supply
every field/curve/hash primitive via their public APIs — no new field,
curve, or hash primitive is implemented here; the RFC's own composition
of them (try-and-increment hash-to-curve, the RFC-8032-style nonce, the
Schnorr-shaped DLEQ challenge/response over `(B, Y)`/`(H, Gamma)`) is
this module's contribution.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Test vectors
------------

RFC 9381 Appendix B.3 publishes three full worked examples for
ECVRF-EDWARDS25519-SHA512-TAI (its own "Example 16/17/18"), each with
every intermediate value this module's public API can independently
produce: `SK`, `PK`, `alpha`, the VRF secret scalar `x`, the
`try_and_increment` counter, `H`, the pre-reduction nonce hash
`k_string`, the reduced nonce `k`, `U = k*B`, `V = k*H`, the 80-byte
proof `pi`, and the 64-byte output `beta`. `kat_vectors.zig` transcribes
all of them (SK/PK/alpha are RFC 8032 §7.1's own Ed25519 test-vector
keys/messages, reused by RFC 9381's Appendix B preamble; every
ECVRF-specific field was generated by the `reyzin/ecvrf` reference
implementation RFC 9381 cites, per Appendix B's own opening sentence —
this module did not consult that implementation itself, only its
published numeric output as printed in the RFC text).

Extraction method (2026-07-16): the vectors were NOT hand-transcribed
digit-by-digit. `rfc9381.txt` was fetched directly from
`https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9381.txt`, and Appendix B.3's text was
parsed with a small Python script that (1) split the section into its
three `Example N:` blocks, (2) located each named field
(`SK`/`PK`/`alpha`/`x`/`H`/`k_string`/`k`/`U`/`V`/`pi`/`beta`) up to the
next field label or end of block, (3) stripped the `k*B =`/`k*H =`
inline annotations on the `U`/`V` lines (their literal `B`/`H` characters
are valid hex digits and would otherwise corrupt the extracted value —
caught and fixed during this pass by a length check: `U`/`V` are ptLen =
32 bytes each; a bare hex-digit scan without stripping the annotation
first produces a spurious 33rd byte), and (4) re-joined each field's
line-wrapped hex digits into one contiguous string. Every extracted
field's byte length was then cross-checked against RFC 9381 §5.5's own
stated widths (`SK`/`PK`/`x`/`H`/`k`/`U`/`V` = 32 bytes each, `k_string`
= 64 bytes, `pi` = `ptLen+cLen+qLen` = 80 bytes, `beta` = `hLen` = 64
bytes) before being embedded in `kat_vectors.zig` — all eleven fields
across all three examples matched their expected width on the first
corrected pass.

Status
------

**Implemented.** `secretScalar`/`publicKey` (RFC 8032 §5.1.5 key
derivation), `encodeToCurve` (§5.4.1.1 try-and-increment),
`nonceGenerationString`/`nonceGeneration` (§5.4.2.2), `decodeProof`
(§5.4.4), `validateKey` (§5.4.5), `prove` (§5.1), `proofToHash` (§5.2),
and `verify` (§5.3) are all real, no stubs. `kat_test.zig` validates the
whole stack byte-exact against RFC 9381 Appendix B.3's three official
vectors — including every published intermediate value, not just the
final `pi`/`beta` — plus tamper/negative tests RFC 9381 does not itself
publish (flipping a byte inside `Gamma`/`c`/`s` independently, wrong
`alpha`, a small-order/non-canonical public key): 20/20 tests pass in
both Debug and ReleaseFast (verified 2026-07-16).
